{
  "dom_id": "step-q102345",
  "name": "Question 5",
  "problem": "Given the orthogonal basis $B$ of ${R}^{3}$ and the vector $x$ below, find the value of $5{x}_{3}$, where ${x}_{3}$ is the third component of $[x]_{B}.$\n\n$$\nB={[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]},\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -7 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]\n$$",
  "worked_solution": "Let's denote the vectors of $B$ as ${q}_{1},\\,{q}_{2},\\,{q}_{3}.$ \n\nSince $B$ is an orthogonal basis, we have that $[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]$, where ${x}_{i}=\\frac{x\\cdot {q}_{i}}{{q}_{i}\\cdot {q}_{i}}.$ Computing ${x}_{3},$ we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{3} & =\\frac{x\\cdot {q}_{3}}{{q}_{3}\\cdot {q}_{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\cdot 2+(-7)\\cdot 1+6\\cdot 0}{{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{10-7+0}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n5{x}_{3}=5\\cdot \\frac{3}{5}=3.\n$$",
